gpl: name the initial-position distribution so it can be measured

STUDY INSTRUMENTATION -- NOT AN UPSTREAM CANDIDATE.

`InitialPlace::placeInstsInitialPositions()` decides where every
placeable instance sits before the conjugate-gradient solve: a region
center, else the ODB location, else the core center. The core-center
rung arrived with the RePlAce import (OpenROAD 456cde4187, 2020-08-11,
"replace rm submodule") carrying the comment "normally, initial place
will place all cells in the centers", and has been reversed four times
since -- #5253, #7644, #9353 upstream, and ORFS bab0134ebd downstream --
without a QoR measurement attached to any of them.

This patch adds `global_placement -initial_position_mode <mode>`, which
replaces the fallback with a named distribution: center, odb, corner_ll,
corner_ur, uniform, gauss_tight, gauss_wide, spread, anchored. It exists
so the arms of a study can be selected at run time from one binary
rather than one build per arm.

An empty mode -- the default, and what every existing caller gets --
takes the shipped code path unchanged, so a build carrying this patch
produces byte-identical results to one without it unless the new key is
passed. That is the property the study's control arm checks.

It carries a `log_->report` line naming the mode, the instance count and
the seed. The harness treats that line as the witness for an arm and
discards a sample whose log does not name the mode it was run under.

Not upstreamed, and not proposed for upstreaming: the mode selector is a
measuring instrument, not a feature. It retires when the study branch
does. If a distribution wins, what goes upstream is that distribution as
the default -- one concern, its own patch, with the numbers attached.

Applies to OpenROAD c7e5e75131a65e402a2d46a1cb82659d725655da.

